<header><p class="enc-kicker">State</p><h1 class="enc-title">Medang Kingdom</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Medang</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Medang kingdom, also known as Mataram, was a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hindu-buddhist">Hindu-Buddhist</a> kingdom of central and later eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/java">Java</a> from the eighth to the early eleventh century. It is associated with the building of monuments such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/borobudur">Borobudur</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prambanan">Prambanan</a>. It was a rival of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/srivijaya">Srivijaya</a>, and conflict between the two shaped the region's politics.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/medang-kingdom.jpg" alt="Medang Kingdom" width="320" height="134" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Gunawan Kartapranata · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Mataram_Kingdom_en.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The earliest account of the kingdom is the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/canggal-inscription">Canggal inscription</a> of 732, found at the Gunung Wukir temple southwest of Magelang. Written in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sanskrit">Sanskrit</a>, it records the erection of a lingga, a symbol of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shiva">Shiva</a>, on the order of King Sanjaya, Rakai of Mataram. It says that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/java">Java</a>, rich in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rice">rice</a> and gold, was ruled by King Sanna, and that after his death the kingdom fell into disunity until Sanjaya, his sister's son, took the throne and conquered the surrounding areas. The kingdom relied heavily on rice farming, using the fertile volcanic soil of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-java">Central Java</a>, and later also benefited from maritime trade.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The period from King Panangkaran to King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/balitung">Balitung</a>, roughly 760 to 910, marked the high point of classical Javanese civilisation, with great temples rising on the Kedu and Kewu plains, among them Sewu, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/borobudur">Borobudur</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prambanan">Prambanan</a>. Sanjaya was a Shaivite, but Panangkaran was a Mahayana Buddhist, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buddhism">Buddhism</a> enjoyed court patronage until the end of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samaratungga">Samaratungga</a>'s reign. Shaivite <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hinduism</a> regained favour under Pikatan. Scholars disagree whether two dynasties, the Shaivite Sanjaya and the Buddhist Shailendra, ruled <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-java">Central Java</a>, or only one.</p><p>Around 929 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mpu-sindok">Mpu Sindok</a> shifted the centre of the kingdom to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-java">East Java</a> and founded the Isyana dynasty. One theory blames an eruption of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mount-merapi">Mount Merapi</a> during the reign of King Wawa, and several temples were found buried under volcanic debris. In 990 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dharmawangsa">Dharmawangsa</a> launched a naval invasion to capture <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/palembang">Palembang</a>, and Chinese accounts record that a Srivijayan envoy learned that his country had been attacked by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/java">Java</a>. In 1017 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassal</a> king of Wurawari attacked Dharmawangsa Teguh, breaking up the Mataram state, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/airlangga">Airlangga</a>'s kingdom of Kahuripan became its leading successor in East Java.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>The bas-reliefs and statues of Mataram's temples, often called Javanese or Shailendra art, are considered among the finest works of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hindu-buddhist">Hindu-Buddhist</a> world, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prambanan">Prambanan</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/borobudur">Borobudur</a> the pinnacle. The Borobudur reliefs hold the most complete rendering of Buddhist sacred texts. The era is hailed as the classical period of Javanese civilisation, when Hindu-Buddhist elements mixed with indigenous ones. Javanese art influenced Srivijayan art in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sumatra">Sumatra</a> and southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thailand">Thailand</a>, and the similarity of the Bakong temple in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cambodia">Cambodia</a> to Borobudur suggests a Javanese influence on early <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khmer-empire">Khmer</a> architecture.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Mataram kingdom” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>When the Javanese kingdom of <b>Medang</b> under Dharmawangsa attacked Sumatra around 990 CE, Srivijaya withstood the assault, and in 1016 the Medang court was destroyed by a rebellion of the vassal ruler of Wurawari, an event many historians attribute to Srivijayan backing or regard as a Srivijayan invasion…</blockquote><a href="/empire/srivijaya-empire">Srivijaya Empire · Military</a></li></ul></section>
